.SH NAME
ktm-mem \- Allocation and raw memory operations.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
import mem;
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Allocation and raw memory operations.
.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS alloc
.EX
void *alloc(u64 size);
.EE
.PP
\fBsize\fR uninitialised bytes; null when out of memory or when \fBsize\fR is too large (such as a negative number passed as a u64).
.SS free
.EX
void free(void *ptr);
.EE
.PP
Free a block from mem.alloc, mem.allocZero or mem.realloc. null is ignored.
.SS size
.EX
u64 size(void *ptr);
.EE
.PP
The size the block was asked for (not the capacity malloc rounded it up to); 0 for null.
.SS set
.EX
void *set(void *dst, i32 ch, u64 len);
.EE
.PP
Set \fBlen\fR bytes at \fBdst\fR to the low byte of \fBch\fR; returns \fBdst\fR\&.
.SS copy
.EX
void *copy(void *dst, void *src, u64 len);
.EE
.PP
Copy \fBlen\fR bytes from \fBsrc\fR to \fBdst\fR, front to back; returns \fBdst\fR\&. Overlapping buffers are not supported: use memmove for those.
.SS allocZero
.EX
void *allocZero(u64 newSize);
.EE
.PP
\fBnew_size\fR zeroed bytes. One argument, the total size, unlike C's calloc(count, size). null when out of memory.
.SS realloc
.EX
void *realloc(void *ptr, u64 newSize);
.EE
.PP
A new block of \fBnew_size\fR bytes holding the start of \fBptr\fR (as much as fits); \fBptr\fR is freed and the result is always a different pointer. mem.realloc(null, n) is mem.alloc(n). When out of memory it returns null and leaves \fBptr\fR as it was, still yours to free.
